comments[4]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T08:28:30.602Z","harness:codex","Duplicate-check evidence: all-status searches for @toon-format/toon, TOON v4 migration, and TOON 4 found storage decisions and prior minor refreshes but no active owner for the 4.0.0 major compatibility decision. npm metadata on 2026-07-26 reports 4.0.0 with no engines field; deferral is for codec/storage compatibility proof, not a claimed engine incompatibility."
  "2026-07-26T18:31:20.767Z","harness:claude-code","Dependency-drift sweep 2026-07-26 (live npm query, whole manifest).\n\nRuntime and toolchain drift, current -> latest:\n\n| package | current | latest | gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| @toon-format/toon | 2.3.1 | 4.1.0 | **two majors** |\n| npm-package-arg | 13.0.2 | 14.0.0 | one major |\n| typescript | 6.0.3 | 7.0.2 | one major |\n| @sentry/node | 10.67.0 | 10.68.0 | patch |\n| typebox | 1.3.7 | 1.3.8 | patch |\n\npnpm audit reports 0 vulnerabilities at every severity (info/low/moderate/\nhigh/critical all zero), and GitHub Dependabot shows 0 open alerts, so none of\nthis is a security exposure. It is a maintenance-currency finding.\n\nWhy the TOON row is the one that matters here rather than the TypeScript row:\nTOON is the default output encoding for the entire agent-facing surface\n(output_default: \"toon\"), so this dependency sits on every read path an agent\ntouches. Published majors are 0, 1, 2 and 4 across 20 versions — the project is\npinned on 2.x while the registry has moved to 4.x, and there is no 3.x, so the\nupgrade is a single two-major step rather than an incremental walk.\n\nThis also intersects an open correctness defect on the same encoder: GH-747\n(pm-hybj) reports that the TOON encoder emits a quoted notes[] scalar its own\ndecoder rejects, making valid tracked items unreadable — the same failure family\nas pm-iqgj (closed), where the strict decoder mis-parsed bracketed tokens and 16\nitem files had to be recovered. Evaluating whether 4.x already fixes the\nencoder/decoder asymmetry is therefore part of this item's scope and may reduce\nit, not just a compatibility exercise.\n\nNo re-file: this evidence is recorded here because pm-5cgm2z already owns the\nTOON 4 evaluation and item-format migration."
  "2026-07-27T05:36:40.096Z","harness:codex","Implementation evidence: adopted registry-latest @toon-format/toon 4.1.0; targeted strict decoder, item validation, calendar rendering, negative-control, and 250-run adversarial property tests pass 65/65. A read/serialize/read sweep accepted all 2,125 tracked TOON item documents with zero mismatches. npm/npx and Bun/bunx installed consumers passed before the codec change and will be rerun from the final packed head."
  "2026-07-27T06:23:00.313Z","harness:codex","Final local evidence 2026-07-27: tracker-linked tests pass for every item; CI=1 pnpm lint passes ESLint, zero jscpd clones, static quality, 100% docstrings, package boundary, token budget, SDK surface/import-cost/transport ratchets; 377 files and 6,448 tests pass at exact 100% statements/branches/functions/lines. Fresh packed acceptance passed npx, Bun SDK import, bunx, an unrelated npm prefix with resolved executable containment, 86-action SDK/CLI completeness with zero unclassified inputs, three provenance dimensions, durable CLI model/effort/role history, and the 88-command package-first dogfood flow. TOON 4.1 passed a 2,127-item storage integrity read, the earlier 2,125-item read/serialize/read corpus sweep, 250-run adversarial properties, and fail-closed negative controls. Secret scan, pnpm audit, GitHub Dependabot/code/secret scanning, open PR inventory, and open-issue PM-link inventory are all clean; only canonically deferred incompatible Sentry, npm-package-arg, and TypeScript upgrades remain. Live pm validate reports zero history drift, zero storage failures, and only the existing 290 historical resolution warnings; pm health is healthy with real-time telemetry draining."
